The '''Adonian International Settlement Bank''' (AISB) is an international financial institution established by the [[Adonian Community]] on March 17, 1954. Headquartered in [[Areza]], the AISB serves as a central bank for the central banks of AC member states, facilitating inter-state settlements, providing banking services to AC institutions, and monitoring financial stability across the Adonian Community. It is one of the principal financial institutions of the Adonian Community, alongside the [[Adonian Monetary Authority]] (AMA).

While the [[Adonian Monetary Authority]] is responsible for issuing and managing the [[International dollar]] and setting monetary policy, the AISB handles the settlement of payments between member state central banks in both International dollars and national currencies, serving as the clearing hub for AC financial transactions. The two institutions operate independently but maintain close institutional cooperation.

== History ==

=== Background ===

The [[Adonian Community]] was established on January 1, 1948, following the [[Treaty of Koblenz (1947)]]. The [[Adonian Monetary Authority]] was created the same year by the [[Treaty of Calatayud (1947)|Treaty of Calatayud]] to issue and manage the [[International dollar]], which entered circulation on September 15, 1948. However, the early AC framework lacked a dedicated institution for settling payments between member state central banks.

During the first six years of the AC's operation, inter-state settlements were handled through bilateral arrangements and ad hoc clearing mechanisms administered by the AMA. As the number of AC member states grew and post-war international trade volumes increased, these arrangements proved increasingly inadequate. Disputes over settlement delays, currency conversion discrepancies, and the lack of a neutral arbitration mechanism for inter-state financial disputes underscored the need for a permanent, centralized settlement institution.

=== Establishment ===

The [[Adonian Assembly]] approved the charter of the Adonian International Settlement Bank in early 1954, and the institution was formally established on March 17, 1954. The AISB was one of the first institutions to be headquartered in [[Areza]], preceding the official inauguration of the city as the AC's administrative seat in April 1954. Initial operations were conducted from temporary facilities in Areza while permanent premises were under construction.

The AISB's founding members comprised the central banks of the original 28 AC member states. The institution's initial mandate focused on three core functions: providing settlement accounts for member central banks, facilitating currency transactions between states, and serving as a depositary for AC institutional funds.

=== Early operations (1954–1960s) ===

In its early years, the AISB operated primarily as a settlement mechanism for bilateral payments between central banks. The institution's first major operational challenge was the transition from the ad hoc clearing arrangements of the 1948–1954 period to a standardized, multilateral settlement system. This transition was completed in 1957 with the launch of the [[Adonian Settlement System]], a platform that allowed central banks to settle payments in both International dollars and national currencies through accounts held with the AISB.

The decolonization wave of the 1960s brought a significant influx of new member states to the Adonian Community, and consequently, new central banks to the AISB. The institution's membership expanded from 28 to over 60 by the end of the decade, requiring upgrades to the settlement system's capacity and the establishment of regional offices to serve new members.

